WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.907
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.207 --> 00:00:09.629
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:15.878
Unit twelve: Healthcare Policy. Today you will choose the right verb after a number of and the number of.

4
00:00:16.178 --> 00:00:18.819
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:23.243
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:26.106
Universal healthcare.

7
00:00:27.117 --> 00:00:31.995
Universal healthcare means a system that provides medical care for all citizens.

8
00:00:32.583 --> 00:00:35.454
Universal healthcare is funded through taxes.

9
00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.608
Your turn. Say: Universal healthcare.

10
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:45.360
Universal healthcare.

11
00:00:46.117 --> 00:00:47.501
Health insurance.

12
00:00:48.517 --> 00:00:53.117
Health insurance means an arrangement in which payments cover the cost of medical care.

13
00:00:53.717 --> 00:00:56.413
Health insurance covers most hospital bills.

14
00:00:57.017 --> 00:01:00.203
Your turn. Say: Health insurance.

15
00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:04.811
Health insurance.

16
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:06.969
Public health system.

17
00:01:07.983 --> 00:01:11.806
Public health system means hospitals and services run by the government.

18
00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:15.542
The public health system treated millions of patients.

19
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:19.534
Your turn. Say: Public health system.

20
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.935
Public health system.

21
00:01:24.683 --> 00:01:25.951
Medical cost.

22
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.925
Medical cost means the money needed to pay for treatment.

23
00:01:30.517 --> 00:01:33.747
A single operation can raise the medical cost sharply.

24
00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:37.142
Your turn. Say: Medical cost.

25
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.326
Medical cost.

26
00:01:42.083 --> 00:01:43.688
Healthcare access.

27
00:01:44.683 --> 00:01:48.912
Healthcare access means the ability to reach and use medical services.

28
00:01:49.517 --> 00:01:52.805
Healthcare access is limited in remote villages.

29
00:01:53.417 --> 00:01:56.557
Your turn. Say: Healthcare access.

30
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.040
Healthcare access.

31
00:02:01.783 --> 00:02:02.993
Preventive care.

32
00:02:03.983 --> 00:02:08.015
Preventive care means medical care that stops illness before it starts.

33
00:02:08.617 --> 00:02:11.499
Preventive care includes vaccination and screening.

34
00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:15.014
Your turn. Say: Preventive care.

35
00:02:17.867 --> 00:02:18.972
Preventive care.

36
00:02:19.717 --> 00:02:21.205
Health disparity.

37
00:02:22.217 --> 00:02:26.085
Health disparity means an unfair difference in health between groups.

38
00:02:26.683 --> 00:02:30.169
A health disparity separates rich and poor districts.

39
00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:34.260
Your turn. Say: Health disparity.

40
00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.380
Health disparity.

41
00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:40.861
Pharmaceutical regulation.

42
00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:46.984
Pharmaceutical regulation means official rules controlling how medicines are made and sold.

43
00:02:47.583 --> 00:02:51.290
Pharmaceutical regulation protects patients from unsafe drugs.

44
00:02:51.883 --> 00:02:55.546
Your turn. Say: Pharmaceutical regulation.

45
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.121
Pharmaceutical regulation.

46
00:03:00.933 --> 00:03:04.619
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:05.483 --> 00:03:11.874
Visal, a report says 'A number of patients are waiting' but 'The number of patients is rising.' Why do the verbs differ?

48
00:03:12.983 --> 00:03:24.671
A number of means 'several' and takes a plural verb. The number of is a single figure, so it takes a singular verb. A small change in the article changes the agreement.

49
00:03:25.783 --> 00:03:27.609
What about uncountable nouns?

50
00:03:28.717 --> 00:03:41.969
We say a great deal of medical cost, a large amount of funding, and little preventive care. Use many and a number of with countable nouns, and much, a great deal of with uncountable nouns.

51
00:03:43.083 --> 00:03:45.629
Can you show a healthcare example?

52
00:03:46.717 --> 00:03:57.133
A great deal of evidence suggests that universal healthcare lowers health disparity, but the amount of money required remains a matter of debate.

53
00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.777
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:00.617 --> 00:04:04.555
Grammar focus: Quantifiers: a number of and the number of.

55
00:04:04.855 --> 00:04:06.065
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.144
A number of patients are waiting for treatment.

57
00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.572
The number of patients is rising every year.

58
00:04:13.483 --> 00:04:17.050
A great deal of evidence suggests that preventive care saves money.

59
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:27.827
A number of + plural verb; the number of + singular verb. Use a great deal of / a large amount of with uncountable nouns, and a large number of with countable ones.

60
00:04:28.583 --> 00:04:32.397
Be careful! Don't say: A number of doctor is needed.

61
00:04:34.333 --> 00:04:37.157
Say: A number of doctors are needed.

62
00:04:37.457 --> 00:04:39.851
A number of takes a plural noun and verb.

63
00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:43.435
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:44.233 --> 00:04:45.699
Who Pays for Health?.

65
00:04:45.999 --> 00:04:49.868
Healthcare is one of the largest expenses in any national budget.

66
00:04:50.867 --> 00:04:59.054
A number of countries offer universal healthcare through a public health system, while the number of people with private health insurance remains high elsewhere.

67
00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:04.702
A great deal of research shows that preventive care reduces long-term medical cost.

68
00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:12.344
Even so, a large amount of money is still needed, and a health disparity often remains between rich and poor areas.

69
00:05:13.333 --> 00:05:18.259
Pharmaceutical regulation, meanwhile, aims to keep medicines both safe and affordable.

70
00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:23.380
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:24.217 --> 00:05:26.274
A number of patients blank waiting.

72
00:05:26.574 --> 00:05:29.845
A, is. B, are. C, was being.

73
00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.330
The answer is B: are.

74
00:05:36.483 --> 00:05:38.634
The number of patients blank rising.

75
00:05:38.934 --> 00:05:42.100
A, are. B, were. C, is.

76
00:05:45.633 --> 00:05:47.772
The answer is C: is.

77
00:05:48.917 --> 00:05:51.764
blank evidence suggests that prevention saves money.

78
00:05:52.064 --> 00:05:55.984
A, A great deal of. B, Many. C, A number of.

79
00:05:59.533 --> 00:06:02.009
The answer is A: A great deal of.

80
00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:04.929
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:05.229 --> 00:06:11.762
A, A number of doctor is needed.. B, The number of doctors are growing.. C, A number of doctors are needed..

82
00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.553
The answer is C: A number of doctors are needed..

83
00:06:19.717 --> 00:06:25.037
According to the reading, pharmaceutical regulation aims to keep medicines safe and blank.

84
00:06:25.337 --> 00:06:29.037
A, affordable. B, secret. C, rare.

85
00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:34.356
The answer is A: affordable.

86
00:06:35.567 --> 00:06:39.032
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:39.883 --> 00:06:44.623
What are the tradeoffs between a universal healthcare system and a private insurance system?

88
00:06:53.417 --> 00:06:54.952
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:55.252 --> 00:07:25.248
A universal system guarantees access for everyone and can control costs by negotiating prices, but it may need high taxes and can create waiting lists. A private insurance system often offers choice and faster treatment for those who can pay, yet it can leave poorer people uncovered and make costs rise. Each model trades equality against flexibility, so societies must decide their priorities.

90
00:07:25.548 --> 00:07:27.420
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:28.317 --> 00:07:33.384
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and quantifiers: a number of and the number of.

92
00:07:33.684 --> 00:07:38.530
Today's value is equity. Make room for the voices that are often left unheard.

93
00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:41.611
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:41.911 --> 00:07:44.783
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

